  PROVENANCE: DERIVED, NOT CAPTURED. No unit test run produced this document. A
  genuine capture was attempted first and is impossible in this checkout on two
  independent grounds. First, no JDK 8 runtime is present, and installing one so
  that the capture merely looks complete is forbidden. Second, the working tree
  already sits past the base commit, so the ordering that requires the capture to
  be taken before any file is edited can no longer be satisfied; that ordering is
  irreversible, which is why it is disclosed here rather than papered over.

  This document therefore ASSERTS NO TEST OUTCOME. The five testcase elements are
  an enumeration of the @Test methods read, in source order, from
  acm-services/acm-service-login/src/test/java/com/armedia/acm/auth/AcmAuthenticationManagerTest.java
  at source lines 94, 128, 159, 189 and 219. The absence of failure and error
  children records that no failure and no error was observed, not that a passing
  run was observed. Every time attribute is 0 because no duration was measured. No
  run host and no run instant attribute is present, because no run host and no run
  instant exist to record. The properties element is deliberately empty: no forked
  JVM system property dump was captured, and none is invented here.

  Reading guard for this class specifically. Three of the five methods are JUnit 4
  expected exception tests, declared @Test(expected = AuthenticationServiceException.class)
  at source lines 159, 189 and 219. Such a method passes when the declared
  exception is thrown and fails only when it is not, so each is an ordinary
  testcase element carrying no failure and no error child. Attaching a failure
  element to any of the three, on the strength of its method name or of the
  exception class its annotation names, would record authentication failures that
  no run reported.

  Provenance for this archive is recorded in the notes folder that accompanies this
  surefire tree, and is not duplicated further into this document. Comparison
  against the corresponding migrated report is judged on testcase presence,
  testcase outcome and failure message content, not on byte identity.
-->
